
=== leg 12 — the leak sweeps (token built at run time, every grep is -q) ===

exit semantics: 1 = clean, 0 = a hit, >=2 = the check itself failed.

--- 12a S1 on this repo, WITHOUT the two path exemptions ---
$ git grep -q --untracked "$TOK" -- .
exit=0 (0 = hit)

--- 12b S1 with the exemption boundary (the two skill paths) ---
$ git grep -q --untracked "$TOK" -- . ":(exclude).claude/skills/kestra-exam/*" ":(exclude)workflow/kestra-exam/*"
exit=1 (1 = clean)

So the exemption is load-bearing: the skill documents the path it forbids
elsewhere, and nothing outside those two paths carries the token — including
this eval, which builds the token at run time for exactly that reason.

--- 12c S2, commit messages, no exemption at all ---
$ git log --all --grep="$TOK" --oneline | wc -l
       0

--- 12d S3, history blobs: zsh unwrapped vs sh -c ---
$ zsh -c <the recipe as written, unwrapped: unquoted $revs>
fatal: unable to resolve revision: cc834eb914911fd1f4d92d7f5914271821e8a455
exit=128 (>=2: the check itself failed, so it never ran)
$ sh -c <the same recipe, guarded and wrapped>
exit=0 (1 = clean, 0 = a hit)

--- 12d-note FINDING: S3 hits on THIS repo while S1 reports clean, because
    the token sits in two TRACKED files that `--untracked` silently skips.
    S3's hit list, paths only, and the commit that added them: ---
idea/flow-final.excalidraw
idea/flow-final.svg
0028bc7 Assemble flow-final: the locked prep-chain design as one diagram

$ git grep -l "$TOK" -- idea/                              # tracked-only
idea/flow-final.excalidraw
idea/flow-final.svg
exit=0 (0 = hit)
$ git grep -l --untracked "$TOK" -- idea/                  # S1 as written
exit=1 (1 = a FALSE clean)
$ git grep -l --untracked --no-exclude-standard "$TOK" -- idea/
idea/flow-final.excalidraw
idea/flow-final.svg
exit=0 (0 = hit, reached again)
$ git ls-files -v idea/flow-final.svg          # H = tracked, not untracked
H idea/flow-final.svg
$ git check-ignore --no-index -v idea/flow-final.svg
/Users/arkaphatp/Documents/HUN/dev/hun-registry-skill/kestra/.git/info/exclude:8:idea/	idea/flow-final.svg

Mechanism, measured end to end: `idea/` is listed in this repo`s local
.git/info/exclude, and the two files are tracked anyway (H). `--untracked`
implies --exclude-standard, whose walk skips excluded paths *including tracked
ones*, so S1 never reads them. `git check-ignore` without --no-index reports
them as NOT ignored (it assumes tracked == not ignored), which is why the
condition is easy to miss by hand.

Consequence for the recipe, sharper than the residual as written: the residual
says --untracked skips gitignored files. Measured, it also skips TRACKED files
under an exclude rule, so S1 can report clean over a committed leak. S1 needs
either --no-exclude-standard (accepting node_modules noise) or a second pass
without --untracked; and on this repo S3 cannot pass until the two diagram
exports are re-exported without the path or added to the exemption list.

--- 12e the case S3 exists for: a leak committed at N, removed at N+2 ---
$ git -C <leakrepo> grep -q --untracked "$TOK" -- .        # S1
exit=1 (1 = clean worktree)
$ git -C <leakrepo> log --all --grep="$TOK" --oneline | wc -l   # S2
       0
$ sh -c <S3 guarded> in <leakrepo>
exit=0 (0 = HIT: the blob is still in history)

--- 12f the same leak inside an exempt path stays clean under S3 ---
S3 with exemption exit=1 (1 = clean)
S3 without exemption exit=0 (0 = hit)

